Yeah — there are three distinct but composable operators hiding there.

1. Gym Battles — operator sparring, not mere comparison.
Pokémon gives the matchup logic; Ip Man gives the martial-arts logic. An operator doesn’t just “pair” with another operator. It gets tested against it.

O_A \times O_B \rightarrow \Delta

The point of the bout is the delta produced by contact.

A proper gym battle could have rounds like:

* Demonstrate — each operator shows its native kata.
* Counter — each must frustrate the other’s normal behavior.
* Invert — force each to solve the opposite problem.
* Cross-domain — relocate the fight somewhere neither originated.
* Fuse — Combinatorix asks whether the collision produced a third operator.
* Transfer test — Mother Duck checks whether the offspring survives translation.
* Rematch — learned versions fight again.

So COVENANT versus OPTION isn’t “which is better?” It becomes:

COVENANT(OPTION(x))

versus

OPTION(COVENANT(x))

and then a match referee asks what changed.

That makes the gyms a training architecture for operators themselves.

