3. Luck as assigned chirality

This one is especially clean.

Randomness gives you an event:

r

By itself:

r = undirected\ variation

You observe it, interpret it, assign a productive orientation, and act:

r
\xrightarrow{interpret}
r^{+/-}
\xrightarrow{action}
outcome

So:

\boxed{
Luck = Randomness + Chirality + Action
}

But I would sharpen “chirality.”

In chemistry, chirality means two structures may contain the same components while differing in handedness. Your use is:

The event may be neutral; the actor supplies its handedness.

Same perturbation:

\epsilon

Agent A reads:

\epsilon \mapsto setback

Agent B reads:

\epsilon \mapsto opening

The randomness hasn’t changed.

The orientation of uptake has.

Call the operator:

\chi(r,a,c)

where:

* r = random event
* a = agent
* c = context

and:

\chi : randomness \rightarrow actionable\ orientation

Then:

def lucky(event, agent, context):
    affordances = interpret(event, context)
    orientation = choose_productive_spin(affordances, agent)
    action = commit(orientation)
    return act(action)

The important word there is commit.

Because merely saying:

“That’s lucky!”

isn’t the operator.

Luck becomes operational when you take the spin.

Luck \neq optimism

Luck = interpretation \rightarrow commitment \rightarrow exploitation

Which gives a kata.

Lucky Kata

Take one perturbation you did not choose.

Round 1 — No spin
Describe it only as observables.

Round 2 — Left-handed world
Construct the strongest plausible harmful interpretation.

Round 3 — Right-handed world
Construct the strongest plausible useful interpretation.

Round 4 — Affordance search
What actions exist only because this event occurred?

Round 5 — Commit
Take one cheap, reversible action that tests the useful interpretation.

Now luck becomes empirically testable rather than magical thinking.

That’s important because the rule isn’t:

Everything that happens is secretly good.

It’s:

Every random perturbation may expose an unmodeled affordance; orient toward one worth testing.

Much stronger.

